    visit: ["Discuss current intake, food rules, behaviors, symptoms, treatment history, and the people already on your care team.", "Identify immediate safety needs and whether medical assessment should happen before outpatient nutrition work.", "Agree on a structured next step and how the dietitian, therapist, physician, and support people will communicate."],
    urgent: ["Fainting, chest pain, irregular heartbeat, confusion, severe weakness, vomiting blood, or inability to keep food or fluids down.", "Self-harm or suicide thoughts, feeling unable to stay safe, or rapidly escalating eating-disorder behaviors.", "A clinician has warned about unstable vital signs, electrolyte problems, or refeeding risk."],
    faq: [{ q: "Do I need to look underweight to get help?", a: "No. Eating disorders occur at every body size, and serious medical complications can be present without a certain appearance." }, { q: "Can I work on weight loss and eating-disorder recovery together?", a: "When an eating disorder is active, intentional weight loss can conflict with recovery and safety. Your coordinated treatment team should guide priorities." }],
    image: "mosaic/dietitian-support.jpg",
    imageAlt: "Supportive conversation with a registered dietitian",
